description:
persona-core/src/audience.rs:60-66:

    if let Some(val) = param.strip_prefix("persona_require_presence=") {
        require_presence = PresenceLevel::from_str(val).unwrap_or(PresenceLevel::None);
    } else if let Some(val) = param.strip_prefix("persona_max_age=") {
        max_age_secs = Some(val.parse::<u64>().map_err(...)?);

The two extension parameters are handled inconsistently, and the security-relevant one is the lenient one. `persona_max_age=notanumber` returns `InvalidMaxAge`; `persona_require_presence=hardwar` (typo), `=Hardware` (wrong case), or `=biometric` (level this build does not know) silently becomes `PresenceLevel::None`.

`PresenceLevel::None` is the weakest level, and persona-grpc/src/service.rs:146 gates issuance on `ext.require_presence > claim.presence`. With `None` that comparison can never fail, so a relying party that typos its audience string gets a token issued against no presence requirement at all and has no way to tell — the request succeeds, the token looks normal.

`PresenceLevel::from_str` already returns `AssuranceError::UnknownPresence`; the value is being thrown away. The test at audience.rs:120-126 pins the current behaviour as intended, so this needs a deliberate decision, not just a code change.

Fix: add an `UnknownPresenceLevel` variant to `AudienceParseError`, propagate with `?`, and update that test. A relying party asking for a presence level this daemon does not implement should get `invalid_argument`, not a downgrade.
